1. Purpose and Central Claim

1.1 Central claim

Electric motors for planetary surface vehicles face a thermal bottleneck. On the Moon, radiation is the only heat-rejection path, and radiator area is launch-mass-limited. Conventional motors designed for Earth convection and then adapted with add-on radiators are suboptimal.

This work develops a coupled electromagnetic–thermal co-optimization framework that treats the motor and its radiator as a single system. The optimizer discovers motor geometries that deliberately sacrifice peak electromagnetic performance to reduce loss density and internal thermal resistance, enabling smaller radiators.

Compared with a naïve adaptation baseline, the co-optimized designs reduce motor-plus-thermal-system mass while maintaining mission energy requirements across Earth, lunar, and Martian drive cycles.

1.2 Formal hypotheses

Let M_{\text{system,seq}} be the best system mass obtained by sequential design: electromagnetic optimization first, then thermal adaptation. Let M_{\text{system,co}} be the best system mass obtained by simultaneous electromagnetic–thermal co-optimization.

Stage 0 hypothesis:

H_0: \quad M_{\text{system,co}} \ge 0.95\,M_{\text{system,seq}}

H_1: \quad M_{\text{system,co}} < 0.95\,M_{\text{system,seq}}

The null hypothesis must be tested on the lunar worst-case radiator condition: radiator facing sunlit regolith at T_{\text{sink,rad}} = 373\ \text{K}, with net radiative flux near 0.32\ \text{kW/m}^2.

The test must use at least 30 independent random seeds or surrogate realizations. If the null hypothesis is not rejected, the central claim fails.

1.3 Failure condition for the central claim

The full central claim fails if:

1. Co-optimization does not yield at least 5% system mass reduction for the lunar worst-case compared with the naïve adaptation baseline, or
2. The co-optimized design violates any of the following in any environment:
   · peak winding hot-spot temperature T_{hs} \le 180^\circ\text{C},
   · mission energy E_{\text{mission,MAEM}} \le E_{\text{mission,naive}},
   · mechanical, demagnetization, or manufacturing constraints.

1.4 In scope

· Parametric electromagnetic design of a common motor core.
· Coupled electromagnetic–thermal modeling.
· Environment-specific thermal adaptation: radiator area, emissivity, solar absorptance, PCM transient buffering, cooling fluid, insulation, sealing, dust mitigation.
· Mission-level energy evaluation.
· Statistical comparison against environment-appropriate baselines.

1.5 Out of scope / deferred

· Free-form topology optimization of rotor/stator geometry.
· Variable-flux memory motors or speculative adaptive magnetic circuits.
· Advanced model predictive control.
· Integrated inverter co-design; the inverter is an external subsystem with its own thermal budget.
· Full multi-failure-mode reliability optimization; reliability is treated as a constraint.

