2. Design, Environment, and Mission Spaces

The architecture is divided into:

· A common electromagnetic core, \mathbf{x}_c.
· Environment-specific thermal skins, \mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}).

\mathbf{x} =
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}_c \\
\mathbf{x}_t(\text{Earth}) \\
\mathbf{x}_t(\text{Moon}) \\
\mathbf{x}_t(\text{Mars})
\end{bmatrix}

2.1 Common electromagnetic core

\mathbf{x}_c =
\begin{bmatrix}
D_{so} & D_{si} & L_{stk} & g & N_p & N_s & \xi_{\text{barrier}} \\
A_{Cu} & k_{fill} & \text{winding layout} & \text{core material} & \text{magnet material} & \text{segmentation spec}
\end{bmatrix}

where:

· D_{so}, D_{si}: stator outer/inner diameter
· L_{stk}: stack length
· g: airgap
· N_p, N_s: pole and slot numbers
· \xi_{\text{barrier}}: rotor flux-barrier parameter vector
· A_{Cu}, k_{fill}: conductor area and slot fill factor
· winding layout: random, symmetric hairpin, or asymmetric hairpin
· core material: electrical steel grade, magnet grade
· segmentation spec: magnet segmentation count and pattern for eddy-current loss control

The flux-barrier vector replaces the single angle \theta_{\text{barrier}} from v0.3:

\xi_{\text{barrier}} =
\left[
N_b, \theta_{b,i}, \tau_{b,i}, t_{rib}, \lambda_i
\right]

where:

· N_b: number of flux barriers per pole
· \theta_{b,i}: barrier end angles
· \tau_{b,i}: barrier thickness ratios
· t_{rib}: minimum rib thickness
· \lambda_i: magnet insertion fraction per barrier

This retains parametric manufacturability while allowing sufficient geometric freedom for saliency optimization.

2.2 Environment-specific thermal skin

For each environment \mathbf{e}:

\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}) =
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}_{\text{cool}} & \mathbf{x}_{\text{rad}} & \mathbf{x}_{\text{PCM}} & \mathbf{x}_{\text{surv}} & \mathbf{x}_{\text{dust}}
\end{bmatrix}

where:

\mathbf{x}_{\text{cool}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{m}_{\text{cool}} & A_{\text{channel}} & \text{fluid type} & \text{sealing spec}
\end{bmatrix}

\mathbf{x}_{\text{rad}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
A_{\text{rad}} & \epsilon_{\text{rad}} & \alpha_{\text{solar}} & \text{architecture type} & \text{deployment factor}
\end{bmatrix}

\mathbf{x}_{\text{PCM}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
t_{\text{PCM}} & \text{PCM material}
\end{bmatrix}

\mathbf{x}_{\text{surv}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
P_{\text{heater}} & t_{\text{insulation}} & \text{lubricant type}
\end{bmatrix}

\mathbf{x}_{\text{dust}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
P_{\text{EDS}} & \text{coating type}
\end{bmatrix}

Discrete architecture choices:

· Earth: pumped liquid loop, water-glycol or dielectric oil, conventional radiator.
· Moon: pumped liquid loop, loop heat pipe, or passive heat pipe; radiator faces deep space, shaded regolith, or sunlit regolith.
· Mars: sealed pumped liquid loop with dust-resistant radiator; optional electrodynamic dust shield (EDS).

Lunar night survival is handled by \mathbf{x}_{\text{surv}}, not by PCM.

