2.4 Lunar radiative environment

The Moon has no convective air. The relevant quantities are:

· Radiative sink temperature seen by the radiator.
· Solar absorptance of the radiator surface.
· Conduction to regolith or vehicle structure.
· View factors to deep space, sunlit regolith, and shaded regolith.

For a radiator at temperature T_{\text{rad}}:

q_{\text{net,rad}} =
\epsilon_{\text{eff}}\,\sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4
-
\alpha_{\text{solar,eff}}\,G_{\text{solar}}
-
\epsilon_{\text{eff}}\,\sigma
\sum_i F_i T_{\text{sink},i}^4

where:

· F_i: view factor to sink i
· T_{\text{sink},i}: deep space 3\ \text{K}, shaded regolith \sim100\ \text{K}, sunlit regolith 100–373\ \text{K}
· G_{\text{solar}}: solar irradiance absorbed by radiator
· \epsilon_{\text{eff}}, \alpha_{\text{solar,eff}}: effective surface properties including dust degradation

Stage 0 uses two extreme cases:

1. Deep-space-facing radiator:
      F_{\text{space}}=1, G_{\text{solar}}=0 if shaded.
      For T_{\text{rad}}=400\ \text{K}, \epsilon=0.9:
   q_{\text{net}} \approx 0.9 \times 5.67\times10^{-8}(400^4 - 3^4)
   \approx 1.31\ \text{kW/m}^2
2. Sunlit-regolith-facing radiator:
      F_{\text{regolith}}=1, T_{\text{sink}}=373\ \text{K}, \alpha_{\text{solar}}=0.3, G_{\text{solar}}=1360\ \text{W/m}^2:
   q_{\text{net}} \approx 0.9\times5.67\times10^{-8}(400^4 - 373^4) - 0.3\times1360
   \approx 0.32\ \text{kW/m}^2 - 0.41\ \text{kW/m}^2
   \approx -0.09\ \text{kW/m}^2

This case illustrates why a radiator facing hot sunlit regolith may be unable to reject heat without careful orientation or sunshades. The optimizer must handle this explicitly.

2.5 Mars dust and fouling

Mars dust is modeled using:

· Effective emittance:

\epsilon_{\text{eff}} = \epsilon_{\text{clean}}(1-D) + \epsilon_{\text{dust}} D

· Effective solar absorptance:

\alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} = \alpha_{\text{solar,clean}}(1-D) + \alpha_{\text{solar,dust}} D

· Fouling resistance:

R_{\text{fouling}} = \frac{\Delta x_{\text{dust}}}{k_{\text{dust}}}

where D is the fractional dust coverage, 0 \le D \le 1.

For the Mars dust-storm case:

· No EDS: D = 0.5
· With EDS: use a first-order cleaning model

D(P_{\text{EDS}}) = D_0 \exp\left(-\frac{P_{\text{EDS}}}{P_{\text{ref}}}\right)

with:

· D_0 = 0.5
· P_{\text{ref}} = 10\ \text{W/m}^2

EDS power adds directly to mission energy:

P_{\text{EDS,total}} = P_{\text{EDS}} A_{\text{rad}}

The effective convective coefficient is:

h_{\text{eff}} = h_{\text{Mars,clean}} \cdot f_{\text{atmosphere}}

where f_{\text{atmosphere}} accounts for dust loading.

