3. Loss and Thermal Models

3.1 Loss model

Total motor loss:

P_{\text{loss}} =
P_{\text{Cu}} + P_{\text{Fe}} + P_{\text{PM}} + P_{\text{mech}} + P_{\text{stray}}

3.1.1 Copper losses with AC effects

P_{\text{Cu}} = \sum_j I_j^2 R_{DC,j} k_{AC,j}

R_{DC,j} = \rho \frac{L_j}{A_{Cu,j}}

k_{AC,j} = 1 + \delta_{\text{skin}}(f_{sw}) + \delta_{\text{prox}}(\text{layout})

If asymmetric hairpin winding is proposed, it must be justified by 2D/3D eddy-current FEA. The validation error against FEA must be less than 5%. In v0.4, asymmetric hairpin is not the default assumption.

3.1.2 Iron losses with temperature dependence

Using Bertotti decomposition:

P_{\text{Fe}} =
m_{\text{Fe}}
\left[
k_h(T) B_m^\alpha f + k_e(T)(B_m f)^2 + k_a(T)(B_m f)^{1.5}
\right]

where k_h(T), k_e(T), k_a(T) are temperature-dependent loss coefficients.

3.1.3 Magnet losses with segmentation

PM loss is computed from transient magnet eddy-current FEA:

P_{\text{PM}} \approx
\int_{V_m} \sigma_m \mathbf{E}_m^2\, dV

For segmented magnets:

P_{\text{PM,seg}} \approx \frac{P_{\text{PM,unseg}}}{N_{\text{seg}}^2}

where N_{\text{seg}} is the number of axial/circumferential segments.

Magnet temperature must satisfy:

T_{\text{magnet}} < T_{\text{demag}}

and cold-start demagnetization:

B_{\min} \ge B_{\text{knee}} \quad \text{at } -40^\circ\text{C and } 8\times\text{ rated current fault}

3.1.4 Mechanical and stray losses

P_{\text{mech}} = P_{\text{bearing}} + P_{\text{windage}} + P_{\text{seal}}

P_{\text{stray}} = k_{\text{stray}} P_{\text{Cu}}, \quad k_{\text{stray}} \approx 0.05\text{–}0.15

3.1.5 Inverter losses

The inverter is modeled as an external heat load:

P_{\text{inv}} = P_{\text{cond}} + P_{\text{sw}}

P_{\text{cond}} = R_{ds(on)} I_{rms}^2

P_{\text{sw}} =
(E_{\text{on}} + E_{\text{off}}) f_{sw}
\left(\frac{V_{dc}}{V_{\text{ref}}}\right)
\left(\frac{I}{I_{\text{ref}}}\right)

The inverter has its own thermal budget and is not integrated into the motor housing in the v0.4 baseline. Inverter losses are included in mission energy:

E_{\text{mission}} =
\int_0^{t_{\text{mission}}}
\left[
P_{\text{loss,motor}}(t)
+ P_{\text{loss,inverter}}(t)
+ P_{\text{pump}}(t)
+ P_{\text{EDS}}(t)
+ P_{\text{survival}}(t)
\right]
dt

where P_{\text{survival}} includes lunar night survival heater power if the heater is part of the motor thermal system.

3.2 Thermal PDE

Governing equation:

\rho c_p \frac{\partial T(\mathbf{r},t)}{\partial t} =
\nabla \cdot \left(k(\mathbf{r})\nabla T(\mathbf{r},t)\right)
+ q_{\text{loss}}(\mathbf{r},t)

for \mathbf{r} \in \Omega.

3.2.1 Earth boundary condition

-k\nabla T \cdot \mathbf{n} =
h_{\text{conv}}(T - T_{\text{amb}})
+
\epsilon\sigma(T^4 - T_{\text{sky}}^4)

where T_{\text{sky}} is the effective sky temperature.

3.2.2 Moon boundary condition

-k\nabla T \cdot \mathbf{n} =
q_{\text{cond}}
+
\epsilon_{\text{eff}}\sigma T^4
-
\alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} G_{\text{solar}}
-
\epsilon_{\text{eff}}\sigma \sum_i F_i T_{\text{sink},i}^4

where:

q_{\text{cond}} =
\frac{k_{\text{interface}}}{L_{\text{interface}}}(T - T_{\text{sink,cond}})

No convection term appears.

3.2.3 Mars boundary condition

-k\nabla T \cdot \mathbf{n} =
h_{\text{total}}(T - T_{\text{amb,Mars}})
+
\epsilon_{\text{eff}}\sigma(T^4 - T_{\text{sky,Mars}}^4)

where:

h_{\text{total}} =
\left(
\frac{1}{h_{\text{eff}}}
+
\frac{\Delta x_{\text{dust}}}{k_{\text{dust}}}
\right)^{-1}

3.3 Radiator sizing and thermal resistance chain

The radiator area is a design variable:

A_{\text{rad}} =
\frac{Q_{\text{reject}}}
{\epsilon_{\text{eff}}\sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4 - \alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} G_{\text{solar}} - \epsilon_{\text{eff}}\sigma T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}}^4}

with:

A_{\text{rad}} \le A_{\max,\text{launch}}

and:

T_{\text{rad}} \le T_{\text{rad,max}}

where T_{\text{rad}} is the radiator surface temperature, and T_{\text{rad,max}} is set by material or coolant degradation.

The full thermal resistance chain is explicit:

T_{\text{hotspot}} =
T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}}
+
Q R_{\text{radiator-to-sink}}
+
Q R_{\text{fluid-to-radiator}}
+
Q R_{\text{motor-to-fluid}}

where:

· R_{\text{radiator-to-sink}} is nonlinear due to radiation.
· R_{\text{fluid-to-radiator}} is the convective/conductive resistance between coolant and radiator surface.
· R_{\text{motor-to-fluid}} includes winding-to-stator, stator-to-coolant, and coolant channel resistances.

This chain must be included in the inner thermal-skin optimization. Without it, the optimizer cannot trade loss density against internal thermal resistance.

3.4 Phase-change material

PCM is used only for transient peak-load buffering, such as a 5–10 minute high-power climb.

Enthalpy formulation:

H(T) =
\int_{T_{\text{ref}}}^{T} c_p(\tau)\,d\tau
+
L f_l(T)

where L is latent heat and f_l \in [0,1] is liquid fraction.

PCM candidate: paraffin SP58, latent heat \approx 200\ \text{kJ/kg}.

PCM is not used for lunar night survival. Night survival is addressed by:

· cold-tolerant insulation,
· bearing/lubricant selection,
· dedicated survival heater powered by non-solar sources.

For lunar night, candidate vacuum grease: Braycote 601EF/602EF, operating range -80^\circ\text{C} to +204^\circ\text{C}.

