4. Electromagnetic and Mechanical Model

Electromagnetic torque:

T_{\text{em}} =
\frac{3}{2} p
\left[
\psi_m i_q + (L_d - L_q) i_d i_q
\right]

where:

· p: pole pairs
· \psi_m: PM flux linkage
· L_d, L_q: dq-axis inductances
· i_d, i_q: dq-axis currents

Field weakening is implemented via standard MTPA/MTPV control with a thermal derating map. It is not a research contribution.

4.1 PMa-SynRM justification

The baseline electromagnetic family is a PM-assisted synchronous reluctance motor with rare-earth-lean magnets.

Key considerations:

· NdFeB remanence temperature coefficient: approximately -0.11\%/\text{K}.
· Ferrite remanence temperature coefficient: approximately +0.2\%/\text{K}, but coercivity degrades at low temperature.
· Ferrite knee flux density can be as low as 0.12\ \text{T} at low temperature.

Therefore, the design must respect:

B_{\min} \ge B_{\text{knee}} \quad \text{at } -40^\circ\text{C and } 8\times\text{ rated current fault}

The parametric flux-barrier geometry is designed to provide high saliency while avoiding demagnetization.

4.2 Mechanical constraints

\sigma_{\max} < \frac{\sigma_{\text{allow}}}{SF}

\omega_{\max} < 0.8\,\omega_{\text{critical}}

Thermal expansion stress is listed as a known limitation in v0.4; it is not yet included in Stage 0.

