5. Optimization Problem

The architecture is a common electromagnetic core with environment-specific thermal skins.

5.1 Inner problem: optimal thermal skin for a given environment

For fixed common core \mathbf{x}_c and environment \mathbf{e}, choose thermal adaptation \mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}) to minimize thermal-system mass:

\mathbf{x}_t^*(\mathbf{e}) =
\arg\min_{\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e})}
M_{\text{thermal}}(\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}))

subject to:

T_{\max}(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}),\mathbf{e}) \le T_{\text{limit}}(\mathbf{e})

A_{\text{rad}} \le A_{\max,\text{launch}}

E_{\text{mission}}(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}),\mathbf{e}) \le E_{\text{naive}}(\mathbf{e})

\text{mechanical, electrical, manufacturing, reliability constraints}

5.2 Outer problem: bi-objective robust design

The outer problem is:

\min_{\mathbf{x}_c}
\left[
M_{\text{motor}}(\mathbf{x}_c) + \max_{\mathbf{e}} M_{\text{thermal}}^*(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e}),
\quad
\frac{1}{|\mathcal{E}|}\sum_{\mathbf{e}} E_{\text{mission}}(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e})
\right]

subject to, for each environment \mathbf{e}:

M_{\text{motor}}(\mathbf{x}_c) + M_{\text{thermal}}^*(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e})
\le 0.90\,M_{\text{system,naive}}(\mathbf{e})

E_{\text{mission}}(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e}) \le E_{\text{naive}}(\mathbf{e})

T_{\max}(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e}) + 10\ \text{K} \le T_{\text{limit}}

plus all other constraints.

This formulation:

· allows each environment to use its own radiator, coolant, insulation, and dust mitigation;
· optimizes the common core against worst-case adapted environment while also reducing average mission energy;
· enforces per-environment success criteria directly;
· remains tractable with parametric geometry and coupled FEA.

5.3 Solver and surrogate strategy

The problem is mixed-integer nonlinear with PDE constraints. Discrete variables are handled by enumerating a small set of material/architecture combinations.

Solver strategy:

· Cheap model: lumped-parameter thermal network + magnetic equivalent circuit.
· Expensive model: 2D/3D FEA electromagnetic + thermal PDE for final validation.
· Surrogate ensemble: Kriging for torque ripple and local accuracy; ANN for efficiency maps; ensemble used for acquisition.
· Optimizer: physics-informed Bayesian optimization with MESA seeding; cheap-expensive multi-objective acquisition.

Physics-informed Bayesian optimization has been shown to reduce optimization time by approximately 45% compared with NSGA-II in motor design. The v0.4 study uses this as the default search strategy.

5.4 Reliability constraints

Simplified reliability constraints:

L_{\text{insulation}} > t_{\text{mission}}

with Arrhenius insulation life:

L_{\text{insulation}} =
L_0
\exp
\left[
\frac{E_a}{k_B}
\left(
\frac{1}{T_{hs}+273}
-
\frac{1}{T_0+273}
\right)
\right]

where:

· L_0 = 20{,}000\ \text{h} at T_0 = 180^\circ\text{C} for Class H insulation
· E_a: activation energy
· T_{hs}: winding hot-spot temperature

Bearing life:

L_{10,\text{bearing}} > 2\,t_{\text{mission}}

with dust-derating for Mars.

Reliability is not optimized as a continuous objective in v0.4.

