6. Baselines and Success Criteria

6.1 Naïve adaptation baseline

The naïve adaptation baseline is:

1. Start from a conventional IPMSM benchmark in the same power class:
   · DOE 2025 100 kW peak / 55 kW continuous benchmark for reproducible FEA.
   · Tesla Model 3 rear motor scaled to the same power class for cost/mass sanity check.
2. Earth: use the production motor with existing water-glycol cooling.
3. Moon/Mars: re-engineer seals, bearings, and lubricants for vacuum/dust, and add a pumped liquid loop or loop heat pipe plus radiator sized for the environment.
4. No electromagnetic re-design is performed.

Tesla sanity check data:

· Specific power: \sim4.6\ \text{kW/kg}
· Motor cost: \sim\$754
· V-shape IPMSM, 48-slot/8-pole
· Magnet mass: \sim1.8\ \text{kg NdFeB}

6.2 Rare-earth critical material metric

R_{\text{critical}} =
\frac{m_{\text{RE}}}{T_{\text{cont}}}
\quad
[\text{g/Nm}]

where:

· m_{\text{RE}}: rare-earth magnet mass
· T_{\text{cont}}: continuous torque at rated speed

6.3 Success criteria

The design is successful if, compared with the naïve adaptation baseline:

1. Pareto dominance:
      The MAEM Pareto front dominates the naïve front in hypervolume indicator by at least 10%:
   HV_{\text{MAEM}} > 1.1\,HV_{\text{naive}}
2. Mass reduction per environment:
   M_{\text{system,MAEM}}(\mathbf{e}) \le 0.90\,M_{\text{system,naive}}(\mathbf{e})
3. Energy constraint:
   E_{\text{mission,MAEM}}(\mathbf{e}) \le E_{\text{mission,naive}}(\mathbf{e})
4. Thermal margin:
   T_{\max,\text{MAEM}}(\mathbf{e}) + 10\ \text{K} \le T_{\text{limit}}
5. Critical-material reduction:
   R_{\text{critical,MAEM}} \le 0.6\,R_{\text{critical,naive}}

These targets are provisional hypotheses, to be tested in simulation. They are not validated results.

