7. Validation Plan and Timeline

Stage 0 — Coupled parametric study

Stage 0 is a standalone publishable study.

Objective: Compare sequential electromagnetic-then-thermal design against simultaneous co-optimization for a single conventional IPMSM under:

· Earth boundary conditions,
· lunar vacuum boundary conditions with the two extreme radiator cases.

Deliverable: First conference paper, e.g., IEEE ECCE.

This validates the methodological claim that co-optimization finds designs missed by sequential methods. It does not yet validate the full central claim against the naïve baseline.

Stage 1 — Naïve baseline reconstruction

Build finite-element and lumped-parameter thermal models of the DOE/Tesla naïve adaptation baseline.

Stage 2 — Coupled multi-environment optimization

Use Bayesian surrogate-assisted optimization to generate Pareto fronts in:

(M_{\text{system}}, E_{\text{mission}}, T_{\max})

Stage 3 — Environment evaluation

Evaluate selected designs on Earth, Moon, and Mars reference missions, including:

· lunar night survival with dedicated survival heater and insulation,
· Mars dust-storm scenario with EDS and fouling.

Stage 4 — Digital prototype

Produce CAD geometry, electromagnetic/thermal/structural simulation, and control-in-the-loop simulation with simple MTPA/MTPV control.

Digital-twin V-model:

· Component: winding AC-loss validation.
· Subsystem: motor + loop heat pipe in thermal vacuum chamber.
· System: Jezero traverse energy simulation.

Stage 5 — Hardware demonstrator

A minimal hardware demonstrator:

· Single stator with interchangeable cooling jackets:
  · water-glycol,
  · sealed liquid loop,
  · heat pipe.
· Validate thermal resistance R_{\text{cond}} + R_{\text{rad}} within 10%.
· Validate:
  · winding AC-loss model,
  · direct dielectric cooling,
  · PCM transient buffering,
  · radiator sizing under vacuum,
  · sealed operation.

---

8. Limitations

Version 0.4 remains a research construct. The most uncertain areas are:

· whether a single common electromagnetic core can be efficiently adapted to Earth, Moon, and Mars;
· whether coupled co-optimization gains are large enough to justify replacing sequential design;
· whether the parametric flux-barrier family is flexible enough for the optimizer;
· whether simplified dust/EDS and reliability models capture dominant failure modes;
· whether thermal expansion stress can be ignored in Stage 0;
· whether the lunar radiator view-factor model is sufficient without full spherical discretization.

---

9. Core Equations and Research Philosophy

The formal optimization problem is:

\min_{\mathbf{x}_c}
\left[
M_{\text{motor}}(\mathbf{x}_c)
+
\max_{\mathbf{e}} M_{\text{thermal}}^*(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e}),
\quad
\frac{1}{|\mathcal{E}|}\sum_{\mathbf{e}} E_{\text{mission}}(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e})
\right]

subject to electromagnetic, thermal, mechanical, environmental, manufacturing, and reliability constraints.

The inner problem is:

\mathbf{x}_t^*(\mathbf{e}) =
\arg\min_{\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e})}
M_{\text{thermal}}(\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}))

for each environment.

The research philosophy is:

Human engineers define the physics, constraints, and manufacturability bounds. Computational optimization discovers the non-intuitive trade-offs within those bounds.

The architecture philosophy is:

Do not optimize the motor for a single specification. Optimize a common motor core and environment-specific thermal adaptation for mission-level performance across Earth, Moon, and Mars.

---

10. Literature Gap

No prior work has been identified that co-optimizes a common motor electromagnetic core with environment-specific radiators under:

· dust-degraded emissivity and solar absorptance,
· launch-envelope radiator area limits,
· lunar view-factor radiative environments,
· rare-earth mass constraints,

across Earth, lunar, and Martian drive cycles.

Existing work optimizes motors for a single environment or treats thermal adaptation as a sequential afterthought.

