Appendix B — Electromagnetic Model Derivations

B.1 PMa-SynRM Torque Equation

The electromagnetic torque of a permanent-magnet-assisted synchronous reluctance motor in the dq reference frame is:

T_{\text{em}} = \frac{3}{2} p \left[ \psi_m i_q + (L_d - L_q) i_d i_q \right]

Derivation.
Starting from the dq-axis flux linkages:

\psi_d = L_d i_d + \psi_m

\psi_q = L_q i_q

The electromagnetic torque is given by:

T_{\text{em}} = \frac{3}{2} p \left( \psi_d i_q - \psi_q i_d \right)

Substituting the flux linkage expressions:

T_{\text{em}} = \frac{3}{2} p \left[ (L_d i_d + \psi_m) i_q - L_q i_q i_d \right]

= \frac{3}{2} p \left[ \psi_m i_q + (L_d - L_q) i_d i_q \right]

The first term is the permanent-magnet torque; the second term is the reluctance torque arising from saliency (L_d < L_q).

B.2 Iron Loss Model with Temperature Dependence

The Bertotti decomposition expresses iron loss as the sum of hysteresis, eddy-current, and anomalous losses:

P_{\text{Fe}} = m_{\text{Fe}} \left[ k_h(T) B_m^\alpha f + k_e(T)(B_m f)^2 + k_a(T)(B_m f)^{1.5} \right]

Temperature dependence of the coefficients is introduced through:

k_h(T) = k_{h,0} \left[ 1 + \beta_h (T - T_0) \right]

k_e(T) = k_{e,0} \left[ 1 + \beta_e (T - T_0) \right]

k_a(T) = k_{a,0} \left[ 1 + \beta_a (T - T_0) \right]

where T_0 = 25^\circ\text{C} and \beta are empirical temperature coefficients. For typical non-oriented electrical steel, \beta_h \approx 0.001\,^\circ\text{C}^{-1}, \beta_e \approx 0.003\,^\circ\text{C}^{-1}, \beta_a \approx 0.001\,^\circ\text{C}^{-1}. These values must be fitted from manufacturer data.

B.3 AC Loss Model and Validation Criterion

The AC loss factor for a conductor is:

k_{AC,j} = 1 + \delta_{\text{skin}}(f_{sw}) + \delta_{\text{prox}}(\text{layout})

Skin effect contribution.
For a single round conductor of radius r_c, the skin effect factor is:

\delta_{\text{skin}} \approx \frac{1}{48} \left( \frac{r_c}{\delta_{\text{skin depth}}} \right)^4

where the skin depth is:

\delta_{\text{skin depth}} = \sqrt{ \frac{2\rho}{\omega \mu_0} }

For rectangular hairpin conductors, the factor is more complex and should be computed from 2D/3D eddy-current FEA.

Proximity effect contribution.
For a winding in a slot, the proximity loss factor can be approximated using the Dowell model:

\delta_{\text{prox}} \approx \frac{1}{3} \left( \frac{h_{\text{conductor}}}{\delta_{\text{skin depth}}} \right)^2 \left( \frac{N_{\text{layers}}^2 - 0.2}{9} \right)

where h_{\text{conductor}} is the conductor height and N_{\text{layers}} is the number of winding layers.

Validation criterion.
If asymmetric hairpin winding is proposed, the analytical/empirical k_{AC} must be validated against 2D/3D eddy-current FEA. The relative error must satisfy:

\frac{ | k_{AC,\text{model}} - k_{AC,\text{FEA}} | }{ k_{AC,\text{FEA}} } < 5\%

If the criterion is not met, the model is rejected.

B.4 PM Eddy-Current Loss with Segmentation Scaling

For an unsegmented magnet, the eddy-current loss is:

P_{\text{PM,unseg}} = \int_{V_m} \sigma_m \mathbf{E}_m^2\, dV

For a magnet segmented into N_{\text{seg}} electrically insulated pieces, the induced current paths are shortened. The loss scales approximately as:

P_{\text{PM,seg}} \approx \frac{P_{\text{PM,unseg}}}{N_{\text{seg}}^2}

Derivation sketch.
The induced eddy-current density magnitude is proportional to the loop dimension l: |J| \propto \omega B l. The power density is p \propto \sigma |J|^2 \propto \sigma \omega^2 B^2 l^2. Integration over volume gives P \propto l^3. If the magnet is divided into N segments, each segment dimension reduces by a factor N in two directions, so the loss reduces by approximately N^2 for axial segmentation. For circumferential segmentation, a similar scaling applies.

A more accurate correction factor is used in simulation:

k_{\text{seg}} = \frac{1}{N_{\text{axial}} N_{\text{circ}}}

where N_{\text{axial}} and N_{\text{circ}} are the number of axial and circumferential segments.

B.5 Demagnetization Constraint and Knee Flux Density

The demagnetization constraint at cold start is:

B_{\min} \ge B_{\text{knee}} \quad \text{at } -40^\circ\text{C and } 8\times\text{ rated current fault}

Knee flux density.
For ferrite magnets, the demagnetization curve at -40^\circ\text{C} shows a knee point at approximately B_{\text{knee}} = 0.12\ \text{T}. At higher temperatures the knee shifts. For NdFeB, the knee is below 0.2\ \text{T} at room temperature, but the remanence decreases with temperature.

Fault current scenario.
The rated current is I_{\text{rated}}. A fault current of 8 I_{\text{rated}} produces an armature reaction field that can locally demagnetize the magnet if the operating point falls below the knee.

The minimum flux density at the magnet surface is computed from FEA under the fault condition. The constraint is enforced for all rotor positions.

