Appendix C — Thermal Model Derivations

C.1 General Heat Equation

The transient heat conduction equation in a solid domain \Omega is:

\rho c_p \frac{\partial T(\mathbf{r},t)}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot \left( k(\mathbf{r}) \nabla T(\mathbf{r},t) \right) + q_{\text{loss}}(\mathbf{r},t)

Boundary conditions.
On the boundary \partial\Omega, the general Robin-type condition is:

-k \nabla T \cdot \mathbf{n} = q_{\text{conv}} + q_{\text{rad}} + q_{\text{cond}}

where each flux is specified per environment.

C.2 Thermal Resistance Chain

The hot-spot temperature is expressed as:

T_{\text{hotspot}} = T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}} + Q R_{\text{radiator-to-sink}} + Q R_{\text{fluid-to-radiator}} + Q R_{\text{motor-to-fluid}}

Derivation.
In steady state, the total heat flow Q passes through three series resistances:

1. Motor internal thermal resistance R_{\text{motor-to-fluid}}: from winding hot spot to coolant bulk temperature.
2. Fluid-to-radiator resistance R_{\text{fluid-to-radiator}}: from coolant to radiator surface.
3. Radiator-to-sink resistance R_{\text{radiator-to-sink}}: from radiator surface to external sink.

For conduction/convection:

R_{\text{cond/conv}} = \frac{1}{h A}

For radiation, linearized around an operating point:

R_{\text{rad}} \approx \frac{1}{4 \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^3 A_{\text{rad}}}

The total temperature difference is:

\Delta T_{\text{total}} = Q \left( R_{\text{motor-to-fluid}} + R_{\text{fluid-to-radiator}} + R_{\text{radiator-to-sink}} \right)

Thus the chain.

C.3 Radiator Sizing Equation

The net radiative heat rejection from a radiator is:

Q_{\text{reject}} = A_{\text{rad}} \left[ \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4 - \alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} G_{\text{solar}} - \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}}^4 \right]

Derivation.
The emitted radiation is \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4. The absorbed solar radiation is \alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} G_{\text{solar}}. The absorbed infrared radiation from the environment is \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}}^4. The net heat rejection is the difference.

Solving for area:

A_{\text{rad}} = \frac{Q_{\text{reject}}}{\epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4 - \alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} G_{\text{solar}} - \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}}^4}

The denominator must be positive; otherwise no heat can be rejected, and the design is infeasible.

C.4 Lunar Radiator View Factor Model

For a radiator with multiple radiative sinks, the net radiative flux is:

q_{\text{net,rad}} = \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4 - \alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} G_{\text{solar}} - \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma \sum_i F_i T_{\text{sink},i}^4

where F_i are view factors satisfying:

\sum_i F_i = 1

For Stage 0, two extreme cases:

· Case A: Radiator faces deep space only: F_{\text{space}}=1, F_{\text{regolith}}=0, G_{\text{solar}}=0 (shaded).
· Case B: Radiator faces sunlit regolith: F_{\text{space}}=0.5, F_{\text{regolith}}=0.5, G_{\text{solar}}=1360\ \text{W/m}^2, T_{\text{sink,regolith}}=373\ \text{K}.

The effective sink temperature T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}} can be defined as:

T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}}^4 = \sum_i F_i T_{\text{sink},i}^4

which simplifies the equation to:

q_{\text{net,rad}} = \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4 - \alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} G_{\text{solar}} - \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma T_{\text{sink,rad,eff}}^4

C.5 PCM Enthalpy Formulation and Smoothing

The enthalpy per unit mass is:

H(T) = \int_{T_{\text{ref}}}^{T} c_p(\tau)\, d\tau + L f_l(T)

where L is latent heat and f_l \in [0,1] is the liquid fraction.

A logistic smoothing function is used for numerical stability:

f_l(T) = \frac{1}{2} \left[ 1 + \tanh\left( \frac{T - T_m}{\Delta T_{\text{phase}}} \right) \right]

where T_m is the melting temperature and \Delta T_{\text{phase}} is the phase-change temperature spread (e.g., 2^\circ\text{C}).

The effective specific heat including latent heat is:

c_{\text{eff}}(T) = \frac{dH}{dT} = c_p(T) + L \frac{d f_l}{dT}

This formulation is used in the thermal network to capture transient PCM buffering.

C.6 Lumped-Parameter Thermal Network

For fast inner optimization, a lumped-parameter thermal network is used:

\mathbf{C} \dot{\mathbf{T}} + \mathbf{G} \mathbf{T} = \mathbf{P}

where:

· \mathbf{C}: diagonal matrix of thermal capacitances,
· \mathbf{G}: conductance matrix,
· \mathbf{T}: node temperatures,
· \mathbf{P}: nodal heat generation.

The network includes nodes for:

· winding hot spot,
· stator tooth,
· stator yoke,
· rotor,
· coolant bulk,
· radiator surface,
· external sink.

Conductance values are derived from geometry and material properties. For radiation, a linearized conductance is used:

G_{\text{rad}} = 4 \epsilon_{\text{eff}} \sigma A_{\text{rad}} T_{\text{operating}}^3

This network enables rapid evaluation of the thermal skin mass and inner optimization.

