Appendix D — Environment and Dust Models

D.1 Lunar Solar and Regolith Sink Model

The lunar solar irradiance is G_{\text{solar}} = 1360\ \text{W/m}^2 (solar constant). The sunlit regolith temperature ranges from 100\ \text{K} to 373\ \text{K}, depending on solar incidence angle and thermal inertia. For worst-case design, T_{\text{sink,regolith}} = 373\ \text{K}.

The shaded regolith temperature is approximately 100\ \text{K}. Deep space is 3\ \text{K}.

A radiator facing sunlit regolith may absorb reflected solar and infrared radiation from the regolith. The model in v0.4 uses the effective sink formulation in Appendix C.4.

D.2 Mars Dust and EDS Model

Effective emittance and absorptance.
Let D be the fractional dust coverage. The effective surface properties are:

\epsilon_{\text{eff}} = \epsilon_{\text{clean}}(1-D) + \epsilon_{\text{dust}} D

\alpha_{\text{solar,eff}} = \alpha_{\text{solar,clean}}(1-D) + \alpha_{\text{solar,dust}} D

Typical values:

· \epsilon_{\text{clean}} = 0.9, \alpha_{\text{solar,clean}} = 0.3
· \epsilon_{\text{dust}} = 0.4, \alpha_{\text{solar,dust}} = 0.6

For the Mars dust-storm scenario, D_0 = 0.5 without cleaning.

EDS cleaning model.
The electrodynamic dust shield (EDS) removes dust using an electric field. A first-order cleaning model:

D(P_{\text{EDS}}) = D_0 \exp\left(-\frac{P_{\text{EDS}}}{P_{\text{ref}}}\right)

where:

· D_0: initial dust coverage,
· P_{\text{EDS}}: EDS power per unit radiator area,
· P_{\text{ref}}: reference power, 10\ \text{W/m}^2.

Total EDS power:

P_{\text{EDS,total}} = P_{\text{EDS}} A_{\text{rad}}

This power is added to mission energy.

Fouling resistance.
The dust layer adds a conductive resistance:

R_{\text{fouling}} = \frac{\Delta x_{\text{dust}}}{k_{\text{dust}}}

Typical dust layer thickness \Delta x_{\text{dust}} = 0.5\ \text{mm}, thermal conductivity k_{\text{dust}} = 0.1\ \text{W/mK}:

R_{\text{fouling}} = \frac{0.5 \times 10^{-3}}{0.1} = 5 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{m}^2\text{K/W}

The total convective resistance is:

h_{\text{total}} = \left( \frac{1}{h_{\text{eff}}} + R_{\text{fouling}} \right)^{-1}

D.3 Earth Sky Temperature Model

For Earth, the effective sky temperature for radiative exchange is often below ambient air temperature, especially at night. A simple model:

T_{\text{sky}} = T_{\text{amb}} - 20^\circ\text{C}

at night, and T_{\text{sky}} = T_{\text{amb}} - 5^\circ\text{C} during cloudy day. For preliminary design, use T_{\text{sky}} = T_{\text{amb}} - 10^\circ\text{C}.

