Appendix F — Optimization Details and Proofs

F.1 Equivalence of Bi-level and Single-Level Robust Optimization

The inner problem is, for fixed \mathbf{x}_c and \mathbf{e}:

\mathbf{x}_t^*(\mathbf{e}) = \arg\min_{\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e})} M_{\text{thermal}}(\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}))
\quad
\text{s.t. constraints}

The outer problem minimizes the worst-case system mass:

\min_{\mathbf{x}_c} \max_{\mathbf{e}} \left[ M_{\text{motor}}(\mathbf{x}_c) + M_{\text{thermal}}^*(\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e}) \right]

Theorem.
The bi-level optimization is equivalent to a single-level optimization with an augmented objective:

\min_{\mathbf{x}_c, \mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}) \forall \mathbf{e}}
\max_{\mathbf{e}}
\left[
M_{\text{motor}}(\mathbf{x}_c) + M_{\text{thermal}}(\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}))
\right]

subject to the same constraints, if the inner problem has a unique solution for each (\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{e}).

Proof sketch.
For any feasible \mathbf{x}_c, define \mathbf{x}_t^*(\mathbf{e}) as the inner minimizer. Then the outer objective equals:

\max_{\mathbf{e}} \left[ M_{\text{motor}} + M_{\text{thermal}}^*(\mathbf{e}) \right]

The single-level optimization over (\mathbf{x}_c,\{\mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e})\}) will choose \mathbf{x}_t(\mathbf{e}) = \mathbf{x}_t^*(\mathbf{e}) for each \mathbf{e}, because any non-optimal \mathbf{x}_t would increase the maximum. Since constraints are separable by environment, the equivalence holds.

F.2 Pareto Optimality and Hypervolume Indicator

The bi-objective outer problem produces a Pareto front in the objective space (M_{\text{system}}, E_{\text{mission}}).

Definition (Pareto dominance).
A design \mathbf{x}_1 dominates \mathbf{x}_2 if:

M_{\text{system}}(\mathbf{x}_1) \le M_{\text{system}}(\mathbf{x}_2)

E_{\text{mission}}(\mathbf{x}_1) \le E_{\text{mission}}(\mathbf{x}_2)

with at least one strict inequality.

Hypervolume indicator.
For a Pareto front P and a reference point \mathbf{r}, the hypervolume is:

HV(P) = \int_{\mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^2} \mathbf{1}\left[ \exists \mathbf{p} \in P: \mathbf{p} \preceq \mathbf{y} \preceq \mathbf{r} \right] d\mathbf{y}

A larger hypervolume indicates a better trade-off front.

F.3 KKT Conditions for the Inner Thermal Skin Optimization

For a fixed \mathbf{x}_c and \mathbf{e}, the inner optimization is:

\min_{\mathbf{x}_t} M_{\text{thermal}}(\mathbf{x}_t)

subject to:

g_1 = T_{\max}(\mathbf{x}_t) - T_{\text{limit}} \le 0

g_2 = A_{\text{rad}}(\mathbf{x}_t) - A_{\max,\text{launch}} \le 0

g_3 = E_{\text{mission}}(\mathbf{x}_t) - E_{\text{naive}} \le 0

KKT conditions.
At a local optimum \mathbf{x}_t^*, there exist Lagrange multipliers \lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3 \ge 0 such that:

\nabla M_{\text{thermal}}(\mathbf{x}_t^*) + \lambda_1 \nabla g_1 + \lambda_2 \nabla g_2 + \lambda_3 \nabla g_3 = 0

\lambda_i g_i = 0, \quad i=1,2,3

These conditions are used to verify that the inner solver has converged to a local optimum.

F.4 Existence of an Optimal Solution for Parametric Geometry

Theorem.
If the design space \mathcal{X} is compact (closed and bounded) and the objective and constraint functions are continuous on \mathcal{X}, then the optimization problem has a global minimum.

Proof.
By the Heine–Borel theorem, a compact set in \mathbb{R}^n is closed and bounded. The continuous functions achieve their minimum on a compact set (Weierstrass theorem). Since the parametric geometry is bounded by manufacturing constraints (rib thickness, diameters, etc.), the design space is compact. The thermal and electromagnetic responses are continuous under parametric variation. Hence a global minimum exists.

F.5 Convergence of Bayesian Optimization

The Bayesian optimization loop with Gaussian process surrogate and upper-confidence-bound (UCB) acquisition has a regret bound:

R(T) \le \mathcal{O} \left( \sqrt{T \gamma_T \log T} \right)

where T is the number of evaluations and \gamma_T is the maximum information gain.

Proof sketch.
Using the GP-UCB algorithm with confidence parameter \beta_t, the cumulative regret is bounded by the sum of confidence intervals at selected points. The sum is bounded by \sqrt{T \gamma_T}. This ensures sublinear regret, i.e., the algorithm converges to the global optimum as T \to \infty.

In practice, the number of FEA evaluations is finite; the bound provides theoretical justification for the surrogate approach.

