Appendix H — Reliability Models

H.1 Arrhenius Insulation Life

The insulation life is estimated using the Arrhenius equation:

L_{\text{insulation}} = L_0 \exp\left[ \frac{E_a}{k_B} \left( \frac{1}{T_{hs}+273} - \frac{1}{T_0+273} \right) \right]

where:

· L_0 = 20{,}000\ \text{h} at T_0 = 180^\circ\text{C} (Class H insulation),
· E_a = 0.9\ \text{eV},
· k_B = 8.617 \times 10^{-5}\ \text{eV/K}.

Derivation.
The Arrhenius rate of thermal degradation is:

k_{\text{deg}} = A \exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{k_B T}\right)

Life is inversely proportional to rate:

L \propto \exp\left(\frac{E_a}{k_B T}\right)

Normalizing at reference temperature T_0 yields the equation.

The constraint is:

L_{\text{insulation}} > t_{\text{mission}}

For a 14-day lunar mission, t_{\text{mission}} \approx 336\ \text{h}. At T_{hs}=180^\circ\text{C}, life is 20,000 h, far exceeding the requirement.

H.2 Bearing L10 Life

The L10 life (10% failure probability) of a rolling-element bearing is given by:

L_{10} = \left( \frac{C}{P_{\text{eq}}} \right)^3 \times 10^6 \quad \text{revolutions}

where:

· C: basic dynamic load rating,
· P_{\text{eq}}: equivalent dynamic bearing load.

For planetary dust environments, the effective load is increased by a contamination factor a_{\text{cont}}:

L_{10,\text{dust}} = a_{\text{cont}} L_{10,\text{clean}}

with a_{\text{cont}} = 0.3–0.7.

The constraint is:

L_{10,\text{bearing}} > 2 t_{\text{mission}}

H.3 Thermal Cycling Fatigue

Thermal cycling on the lunar surface causes solder joint and material fatigue. A Coffin-Manson model is used for critical solder joints:

N_f = C (\Delta \epsilon_p)^{-m}

where \Delta \epsilon_p is the plastic strain range per cycle. This is noted but not fully integrated in v0.4.

