Appendix I — Manufacturing Constraints and Cost Model

I.1 Manufacturing Bounds Rationale

Parameter Bound Reason
Minimum rib thickness 0.8\ \text{mm} Stamping tool wear and structural integrity
Magnet tolerance \pm0.05\ \text{mm} Assembly and demagnetization margin
Slot fill factor hairpin 0.60–0.75 Winding insertion without insulation damage
Slot fill factor random 0.40–0.50 Manual/machine winding feasibility
Minimum airgap 0.5\ \text{mm} Mechanical clearance and assembly
Minimum channel width 2\ \text{mm} Coolant pressure drop and clogging

I.2 Rare-Earth Critical Material Metric

The critical material metric is defined as:

R_{\text{critical}} = \frac{m_{\text{RE}}}{T_{\text{cont}}}

Derivation.
Continuous torque is a primary performance indicator for traction motors. Rare-earth magnet mass m_{\text{RE}} drives cost and supply risk. The ratio measures critical-material usage per unit of useful continuous torque output. Lower is better.

For the Tesla baseline: m_{\text{RE}} = 1.8\ \text{kg}, T_{\text{cont}} \approx 150\ \text{Nm}:

R_{\text{critical,naive}} \approx \frac{1800\ \text{g}}{150\ \text{Nm}} = 12\ \text{g/Nm}

The target is R_{\text{critical,MAEM}} \le 0.6 \times 12 = 7.2\ \text{g/Nm}.

I.3 Rare-Earth Magnet Cost Model

A simplified cost model:

C_{\text{magnet}} = c_{\text{NdFeB}} \times m_{\text{RE}} + c_{\text{ferrite}} \times m_{\text{ferrite}}

where c_{\text{NdFeB}} \approx 80\ \text{USD/kg}, c_{\text{ferrite}} \approx 10\ \text{USD/kg}.

This cost is not a formal objective in v0.4 but is used for reporting.

