Appendix J — Mission and Vehicle Model Details

J.1 Drive Cycle Time Series Generation

The Earth WLTP-class drive cycle is synthesized as a speed-time profile:

\omega_{\text{vehicle}}(t) = \begin{cases}
\text{WLTP profile}, & t < t_{\text{WLTP}} \\
\text{constant 10\% grade climb}, & t_{\text{WLTP}} \le t < t_{\text{end}}
\end{cases}

The lunar traverse is generated as an intermittent sequence of driving and idle periods totaling 100 km over 55 hours of actual driving.

The Mars traverse is a 15 km path with terrain obstacles and a dust-storm segment.

J.2 Vehicle Dynamics and Tractive Energy

The tractive force required at the wheel is:

F_{\text{traction}} = m_v g \sin\theta + m_v g C_{rr} \cos\theta + \frac{1}{2}\rho_{\text{air}} C_d A_f v^2 + m_v \frac{dv}{dt}

where:

· m_v: vehicle mass,
· g: gravitational acceleration,
· \theta: slope angle,
· C_{rr}: rolling resistance coefficient,
· C_d: drag coefficient,
· A_f: frontal area,
· \rho_{\text{air}}: atmospheric density (zero on Moon).

For the Moon, \rho_{\text{air}} = 0, and gravity is reduced. For Mars, \rho_{\text{air}} \approx 0.02\ \text{kg/m}^3.

J.3 Motor Efficiency Map Interpolation

The motor efficiency map is precomputed from FEA for a grid of torque and speed:

\eta_{\text{motor}}(T_{\text{em}}, \omega) = \frac{P_{\text{mech}}}{P_{\text{mech}} + P_{\text{loss}}}

During mission simulation, the operating point is mapped to the map and losses are interpolated linearly.

J.4 Lunar Night Survival Energy

The survival heater power is:

P_{\text{survival}} = \frac{T_{\text{target}} - T_{\text{amb,night}}}{R_{\text{insulation}}}

where R_{\text{insulation}} is the thermal resistance of the insulation blanket. The total survival energy over the 14-day night is:

E_{\text{survival}} = P_{\text{survival}} \times t_{\text{night}}

with t_{\text{night}} = 336\ \text{h}.

