Appendix K — Stage 0 Protocol and Statistical Tests

K.1 Experiment Design

Goal: Compare sequential electromagnetic-then-thermal design vs simultaneous co-optimization for a single IPMSM under Earth and lunar boundary conditions.

Design space: Same parametric geometry family. Sequential method optimizes \mathbf{x}_c for electromagnetic torque density first, then optimizes \mathbf{x}_t for each environment. Co-optimization optimizes (\mathbf{x}_c,\mathbf{x}_t) jointly.

Evaluations: At least 30 independent random seeds for each method.

K.2 Sequential and Co-Optimization Algorithms

Sequential:

1. Maximize torque per mass or minimize active mass for required torque envelope:
      \min_{\mathbf{x}_c} M_{\text{motor}} s.t. torque-speed constraints.
2. For fixed \mathbf{x}_c, optimize thermal skin:
      \min_{\mathbf{x}_t} M_{\text{thermal}} s.t. thermal constraints.
3. Compute total M_{\text{system,seq}}.

Co-optimization:

1. For each seed, optimize both \mathbf{x}_c and \mathbf{x}_t jointly to minimize total M_{\text{system}}.
2. Compute total M_{\text{system,co}}.

K.3 Statistical Hypothesis Testing

Let X_i be the system mass from sequential method, Y_i from co-optimization.

Paired t-test.
Compute differences D_i = X_i - Y_i. Test:

H_0: \mu_D \le 0.05\, \bar{X}

H_1: \mu_D > 0.05\, \bar{X}

using a one-sided paired t-test at \alpha = 0.05.

Wilcoxon signed-rank test.
If normality is violated, use the nonparametric Wilcoxon signed-rank test.

The null hypothesis is rejected if the p-value < 0.05 and the mean difference exceeds 5%.

K.4 Minimum Effect Size and Power

For a power of 0.8 and \alpha = 0.05, with 30 paired samples, the minimum detectable effect size is approximately:

d_{\min} \approx \frac{2.5 \sigma}{\sqrt{n}}

If the expected difference is at least 5% of the mean system mass, the study has sufficient power.

